<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bangabandhu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awami-league">Awami League</a> and the founding leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. After his party won the 1970 Pakistani election but was denied office, the army cracked down and he was arrested as independence was declared in 1971. Returning in 1972, he served as prime minister and then president, until he was assassinated with most of his family in a military coup in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman.jpg" alt="Sheikh Mujibur Rahman" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Press Information Department · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Portrait_of_Bangabandhu_Sheikh_Mujibur_Rahman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mujib was born on 17 March 1920 in the village of Tungipara in the Faridpur district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, into a Bengali Muslim family of small landlords. His father was a law clerk in the courthouse of Gopalganj. He became a student activist, and in 1943 was elected a councillor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>. At Islamia College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> he worked under his mentor Huseyn Shaheed Suhrawardy and campaigned for inclusion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in the 1947 referendum. After partition he studied law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, from which he was expelled in 1949 for inciting employees against the university.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1948 Mujib founded the East Pakistan Students' League and was drawn into the Bengali language movement, which opposed making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> the sole national language. In 1949 he became a joint secretary of the Awami Muslim League, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awami-league">Awami League</a>, and after Suhrawardy's death in 1963 its general secretary. He was elected to the East Bengal Legislative Assembly in 1954 and briefly served in the cabinet of A. K. Fazlul Huq. He spent many years in prison. The government of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> arrested him in 1967 in the Agartala Conspiracy Case, accusing him of plotting with Indian agents. Mass protests forced it to drop the charges on 22 February 1969, and he was then given the title Bangabandhu, Friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>In 1970 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awami-league">Awami League</a> won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>'s first general election, but the military government of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-khan">Yahya Khan</a> refused to transfer power. On 7 March 1971 Mujib gave a speech that called vaguely for independence. The army arrested him late on 25 March and began a crackdown on Bengali civilians. In the early hours of 26 March the Proclamation of Bangladeshi Independence was issued in his name, and nationalists declared him head of the provisional government while he was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Mujib returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> in January 1972. His government drew up a secular constitution, held the first general election and normalised relations with most countries. In 1974 he gave the first speech in Bengali to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a>. The country suffered a severe famine in 1974, and the government formed a paramilitary force, the Jatiya Rakkhi Bahini, against a left-wing insurgency; it was later accused of human rights abuses. In 1975 he introduced a one-party system as the Second Revolution and became president. He was assassinated with most of his family at his Dhanmondi residence in a military coup on 15 August 1975. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has listed his speech of 7 March 1971 in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Register</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sheikh Mujibur Rahman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Joy&quot; and Shibdas Bandyopadhyay's &quot;Ek Din Shurjer Bhor&quot;, recorded by the Calcutta Youth Choir during the Bangladesh War of Independence of 1971, became widely sung, the latter a favourite of <b>Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</b>; in Kerala it became &quot;Nammal Vijayikkum&quot;, the struggle song of the Students Federation of India.</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Pakistani army's crackdown in Dhaka, independence was proclaimed in the name of <b>Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Grievances over language, economics and political power grew, and when the Awami League under <b>Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</b> won the 1970 election but was denied office, the Pakistani army cracked down in March 1971.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tungipara Upazila, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.9,89.88&amp;year=1955" aria-label="Tungipara Upazila on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6952 1319 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sheikh Mujibur Rahman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7402 1589h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7402 1589h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.9,89.88&amp;year=1955">What was here in 1955? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
